@* ForgotPassword.razor — Phase 1.4
   Static-rendered "Forgot Password" page. User enters their email;
   we generate a password-reset token via Identity and (in dev) log
   the reset URL — in production this is where SMTP would fire.

   WHY STATIC SSR (not a Blazor circuit):
     Same reason as Login.razor — the page handles a form POST and
     re-renders the result in the same HTTP response. A Blazor
     circuit would buffer the response and add round-trips without
     benefit. Static SSR keeps the flow to a single request:
       GET /Account/ForgotPassword → render form
       POST /Account/ForgotPassword → process + render "check your
            email" confirmation message.

   ENUMERATION DEFENSE:
     We MUST return the SAME response whether the email is registered
     or not. Otherwise an attacker can submit emails and observe the
     response to enumerate valid addresses. The confirmation message
     is therefore: "If your email is registered with us, a password
     reset link has been sent." This is true even when no email was
     actually sent (because the address wasn't found or the user is
     deactivated).

   EMAIL DELIVERY (v1 = NO SMTP):
     appsettings.json has no SMTP configuration. For now, when a
     token IS generated, we LOG the reset URL at Warning level so
     the developer can grab it from the console and paste it into
     the browser. When SMTP is added later, replace the log call
     with IEmailSender.SendEmailAsync(...).

   FLOW:
     1. User clicks "Forgot password?" link on the Login page.
     2. Lands here, enters their email.
     3. On submit, we call IUserAccountService.GeneratePasswordResetTokenAsync(email).
        - Returns null for "not found" / "deactivated" (no signal leaked).
        - Returns the token string for valid users.
     4. If token is non-null, build the reset URL and log it (dev).
     5. Show the generic confirmation message regardless of step 4.
     6. User clicks the link in the (logged) email → /Account/ResetPassword.

   ANTI-FORGERY:
     EditForm emits the antiforgery token automatically. The
     UseAntiforgery middleware in Program.cs validates it on POST. *@

@page "/Account/ForgotPassword"
@layout LoginLayout
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Interfaces
@inject IUserAccountService UserAccountService
@inject ILogger<ForgotPassword> Logger
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject IHttpContextAccessor HttpContextAccessor
@inject IStringLocalizer<ForgotPassword> Loc

<PageTitle>@Loc["PageTitle"]</PageTitle>

<RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="1rem" Style="max-width: 360px; margin: 0 auto;">
    @* --- Confirmation banner (shown after form submit, regardless of email validity) ---
       NOTE on `AlertStyle`: this is the correct API for RadzenAlert in
       Radzen.Blazor v11.1.1 (verified via official docs + ERP reference).
       Both the property name and the enum type are `AlertStyle` in the
       `Radzen` namespace. Values include Success, Info, Danger, Warning. *@
    @if (_showConfirmation)
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@Loc["ConfirmationTitle"]"
                     Text="@Loc["ConfirmationMessage"]"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Success"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true" />
    }

    @* --- Error banner (only shown on unexpected infrastructure failure) --- *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_errorMessage))
    {
        <RadzenAlert Title="@Loc["ErrorTitle"]"
                     Text="@_errorMessage"
                     AlertStyle="AlertStyle.Danger"
                     Variant="Variant.Filled"
                     ShowIcon="true" />
    }

    @* --- Intro text + form ---
       EditForm + FormName + [SupplyParameterFromForm] is the canonical
       Blazor static-SSR form pattern (same as Login.razor).

       The form is HIDDEN once the confirmation banner shows. Reason:
       the user has already submitted — letting them re-submit a
       different email keeps the flow moving but adds visual clutter
       (two banners + the form). Hiding the form keeps the post-submit
       UI clean: just the green confirmation + the "back to login" link. *@
    @if (!_showConfirmation)
    {
        <RadzenText Text="@Loc["IntroText"]"
                    Style="color: var(--rz-text-secondary-color); font-size: 0.9rem;" />

        <EditForm Model="@FormModel" OnValidSubmit="@HandleForgotPasswordAsync" FormName="forgotPasswordForm">
            <DataAnnotationsValidator />

            <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Vertical" Gap="0.75rem">
                @* NOTE on `autofocus` + `FormModel!`:
                   Same pattern as Login.razor — see that file for the full
                   explanation. Summary:
                     1. `RadzenTextBox` has NO `AutoFocus` parameter in
                        Radzen.Blazor v11.1.1, so the bare `autofocus`
                        HTML attribute is the only way to autofocus on a
                        static-SSR page (OnAfterRenderAsync + FocusAsync
                        doesn't fire here).
                     2. `FormModel` is `ForgotPasswordModel?` (nullable so
                        [SupplyParameterFromForm] can leave it null on the
                        initial GET). `OnInitialized` assigns a fresh
                        instance, but the compiler's nullable analysis
                        can't see that — `!` suppresses CS8602. *@
                <RadzenFormField Text="@Loc["EmailLabel"]">
                    <RadzenTextBox @bind-Value="FormModel!.Email"
                                   Placeholder="@Loc["EmailPlaceholder"]"
                                   Type="email"
                                   autocomplete="email"
                                   autofocus />
                </RadzenFormField>

                <RadzenButton Text="@Loc["SubmitButton"]"
                              ButtonType="ButtonType.Submit"
                              ButtonStyle="ButtonStyle.Primary"
                              Variant="Variant.Filled"
                              Icon="lock_reset"
                              Disabled="@_isSubmitting"
                              Style="width: 100%;" />
            </RadzenStack>
        </EditForm>
    }

    @* --- Back to login link --- *@
    <RadzenStack Orientation="Orientation.Horizontal"
                 JustifyContent="JustifyContent.Center"
                 Style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
        <RadzenLink Text="@Loc["BackToLogin"]"
                    Path="/Account/Login"
                    Icon="arrow_back" />
    </RadzenStack>
</RadzenStack>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Form model. Uses [EmailAddress] + [Required] for client-side
    /// validation. The actual email-existence check happens in
    /// IUserAccountService.GeneratePasswordResetTokenAsync, but its
    /// result is NEVER surfaced to the user (enumeration defense).
    /// </summary>
    private sealed class ForgotPasswordModel
    {
        [System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.Required(ErrorMessage = "Email is required.")]
        [System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations.EmailAddress(ErrorMessage = "Please enter a valid email address.")]
        public string Email { get; set; } = string.Empty;
    }

    [SupplyParameterFromForm]
    private ForgotPasswordModel? FormModel { get; set; }

    private bool _showConfirmation;
    private string _errorMessage = string.Empty;
    private bool _isSubmitting;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        FormModel ??= new ForgotPasswordModel();

        // If the user is already authenticated, they have no business on
        // the Forgot Password page — redirect them to the home page.
        // (Same pattern as Login.razor's already-authenticated handling.
        // A redirect is cleaner UX than showing a "you're already signed
        // in" banner — they didn't intend to land here, so move them on.)
        var httpContext = HttpContextAccessor.HttpContext;
        if (httpContext?.User?.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true)
        {
            Navigation.NavigateTo("/", forceLoad: true);
            return;
        }
    }

    private async Task HandleForgotPasswordAsync()
    {
        var model = FormModel!;
        _isSubmitting = true;
        _errorMessage = string.Empty;

        try
        {
            // ----------------------------------------------------------
            // Generate the reset token (returns null if user not found
            // or deactivated — we treat both identically).
            // ----------------------------------------------------------
            var token = await UserAccountService.GeneratePasswordResetTokenAsync(model.Email);

            // ----------------------------------------------------------
            // If we got a token, build the reset URL and log it (dev).
            // In production, this is where IEmailSender.SendEmailAsync
            // would fire — but appsettings.json currently has no SMTP
            // section, so we log instead. The log message is at
            // Warning level so it stands out in the console.
            //
            // SECURITY NOTE: this log line MUST be removed (or gated
            // behind IsDevelopment()) before deploying to production.
            // Logging reset tokens in production would let anyone with
            // log access hijack any account. We're keeping it now
            // because SMTP isn't wired up and the dev needs SOME way
            // to test the flow.
            // ----------------------------------------------------------
            if (token is not null)
            {
                var resetUrl = BuildResetUrl(model.Email, token);
                Logger.LogWarning(
                    "DEV-ONLY password reset URL for '{Email}': {Url}\n" +
                    "This log line is for development only — remove before production deployment " +
                    "(wire up SMTP + IEmailSender.SendEmailAsync instead).",
                    model.Email, resetUrl);
            }

            // ALWAYS show the confirmation message — even if token is
            // null. This is the enumeration defense: an attacker probing
            // emails should see identical responses.
            _showConfirmation = true;
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            // Defensive: log full exception for ops, show generic message
            // to user. Don't leak exception details.
            Logger.LogError(ex,
                "ForgotPassword: unexpected error generating reset token for email '{Email}'.",
                model.Email);
            _errorMessage = Loc["Error_Unexpected"];
        }
        finally
        {
            _isSubmitting = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Builds the reset URL: /Account/ResetPassword?email=...&amp;token=...
    ///
    /// The token from Identity's DataProtectorTokenProvider contains
    /// characters that aren't URL-safe (+, /, =). QueryHelpers.AddQueryString
    /// URL-encodes both the email and the token via Uri.EscapeDataString
    /// internally, so they survive the round-trip through the browser,
    /// query string, and back into the ResetPassword page's
    /// [SupplyParameterFromQuery] properties (which auto-decode).
    ///
    /// We use NavigationManager.ToAbsoluteUri to get the app's base URL
    /// so the link works whether the app is hosted at the domain root
    /// or under a sub-path.
    /// </summary>
    private string BuildResetUrl(string email, string token)
    {
        var baseUri = Navigation.ToAbsoluteUri("/Account/ResetPassword");
        var path = baseUri.GetLeftPart(UriPartial.Path);
        return QueryHelpers.AddQueryString(path, new Dictionary<string, string?>
        {
            ["email"] = email,
            ["token"] = token
        });
    }
}